\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces No static policy is right everywhere. Four regimes (deadline $\times $ backbone quality), four policies, ten paired seeds each; every cell reports the accuracy gap to the best policy in that regime (percentage points), the delivered update mass (terminal-window delivered capacity, \% of the regime best), and the timely-delivery ratio (\% of best). Fixed $r{=}4$ holds the timely lead everywhere yet pays about 1.5 points in the weak regimes and never delivers more than three quarters of the best capacity; fixed $r{=}16$ owns the nominal regimes but loses 35\% of its deliveries at stress; the annealed schedule matches FedRAS on the strong backbone---low rank is adequate exactly when capacity is free---and drops 1.4--1.5 points precisely where capacity matters. FedRAS is the only policy whose worst cell is bounded at 0.41 points while holding the full delivered capacity of its regime throughout, because it reads which regime it is in from the link instead of fixing the answer offline.}}{15}{table.105}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:failures}{{4}{15}{No static policy is right everywhere. Four regimes (deadline $\times $ backbone quality), four policies, ten paired seeds each; every cell reports the accuracy gap to the best policy in that regime (percentage points), the delivered update mass (terminal-window delivered capacity, \% of the regime best), and the timely-delivery ratio (\% of best). Fixed $r{=}4$ holds the timely lead everywhere yet pays about 1.5 points in the weak regimes and never delivers more than three quarters of the best capacity; fixed $r{=}16$ owns the nominal regimes but loses 35\% of its deliveries at stress; the annealed schedule matches FedRAS on the strong backbone---low rank is adequate exactly when capacity is free---and drops 1.4--1.5 points precisely where capacity matters. FedRAS is the only policy whose worst cell is bounded at 0.41 points while holding the full delivered capacity of its regime throughout, because it reads which regime it is in from the link instead of fixing the answer offline}{table.105}{}}
\newlabel{tab:failures@cref}{{[table][4][]4}{[1][14][]15}}
